WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:04.840
Pet Sematary is the book that Stephen King
thought might be too horrific to publish.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:10.110
Its adaptation in a terrifying film in 1989
further cemented its place among the King

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.790
canon, and now we have an all-new adaptation.

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:16.000
But what about this version of Pet Sematary
sets it apart?

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:20.780
For many Stephen King fans, there are non-scary
books, there are scary books, and then there's

00:00:20.789 --> 00:00:21.900
Pet Sematary.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:26.259
The novel follows the Creed family as they
move to rural Maine so that the patriarch,

00:00:26.259 --> 00:00:30.509
Louis, can take a job as the head of the University
of Maine's medical service.

00:00:30.509 --> 00:00:34.720
Their new house is beside a road frequented
by fast, heavy trucks that often claim the

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:35.860
lives of local pets.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:40.910
As a result, neighborhood children have established
a pet cemetery in the woods, complete with

00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:43.680
the misspelled sign that makes up the book's
title.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:47.250
When his daughter Ellie's cat, Church, is
run over and killed, Louis and his neighbor

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:52.130
Jud Crandall bury the cat near the cemetery…
but Church soon returns to the Creed family

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:53.130
home.

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:55.700
However, something's not right about the resurrected
family cat.

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.720
"Jesus Christ.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:01.080
Dammit."

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:05.600
Just as Jud begins to regret bringing the
cat back to life, another tragedy strikes

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:06.600
the family.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.729
The Creeds' youngest son, Gage, is hit by
another speeding truck, and Louis, shaken

00:01:10.729 --> 00:01:15.720
by grief and his new understanding of the
pet cemetery, begins to ponder the unthinkable.

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:20.760
Technically, the "pet sematary" of the novel's
title isn't the actual supernatural part of

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:21.770
the story.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:26.380
That honor goes to what Jud calls "the real
cemetery," a burial ground set up at the back

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:27.509
of the pet cemetery.

00:01:27.509 --> 00:01:31.740
That burial ground was built by the local
Native American tribe, the Micmacs.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:35.280
When Church dies, Louis chisels out a hole
for him in the burial ground.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:39.429
Jud explains that he once did the same thing
with his own dog, Spot, who was also never

00:01:39.429 --> 00:01:41.380
quite the same after coming back.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:43.180
This leads to Jud's lesson for Louis:

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.000
"Sometimes, dead is better.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.160
The person you put up there ain't the person
that comes back."

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:55.420
Later in the novel, King reveals that the
burial ground was at some point used for bodies

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:59.709
partially consumed by cannibals, and that
a legendary supernatural creature called the

00:01:59.709 --> 00:02:01.869
Wendigo has made a home there.

00:02:01.869 --> 00:02:06.139
Jud says the ground went sour and now anything
buried might come back as a hostile shell

00:02:06.139 --> 00:02:07.499
of its former self.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:10.360
Of course, none of this stops Louis after
his son is killed.

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:15.300
King first conceived of Pet Sematary when
he found himself in a very similar situation

00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:16.310
to Louis Creed's.

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:20.720
He and his family moved to a rural home near
the University of Maine, with a similarly

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:23.410
dangerous road and local pet cemetery.

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:27.700
His daughter's cat was killed by one of the
trucks, and her resulting grief over the incident

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:31.860
helped inspire him to ponder what might happen
if you could bring a loved one back.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:35.320
As King told Charlie Rose in a 1993 interview:

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:36.930
"Here's something that we don't talk about.

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:38.920
People sometimes have kids who die.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:43.890
There are terrible things that happen and
sometimes a child will die young."

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:48.260
By the time he'd finished the novel, King
thought Pet Sematary was simply too dark to

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:49.260
publish.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:52.170
He put the novel in a drawer and believed
it would never be released to the public in

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:53.170
his lifetime.

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:57.040
However, the book was ultimately pulled out
and submitted to finish out King's contract

00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:59.090
with his former publisher Doubleday.

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:03.710
It was a success, and is still among King's
most widely read and celebrated books, made

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:06.380
all the more infamous because of King's own
misgivings.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.140
The 1980s were a particularly fertile time
for King's works.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:15.410
Six years after its publication, Pet Sematary
was made into a film, with King himself providing

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:19.530
the screenplay and insisting that the movie
be shot in his home state of Maine.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:23.540
The film was a box office success and has
gone on to be revered as one of the best Stephen

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:25.310
King adaptations.

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:30.260
In 1992, director Mary Lambert returned to
direct Pet Sematary Two, a film following

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:34.510
a different family and their horrific experiences
with the Micmac burial ground.

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:38.910
However, the sequel was not as successful
as its predecessor, and King successfully

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:41.960
lobbied the studio to have his name removed
from the project.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:43.740
"Uh oh.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:45.960
Looks like daddy got a boo-boo."

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:50.960
In the years since Lambert's film was released,
Pet Sematary has retained its reputation as

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:52.940
one of King's most frightening stories.

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:56.540
It's not surprising that someone would eventually
want to make an updated film adaptation.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:04:02.020
Word of a new version of Pet Sematary first
began to bubble up back in 2010, when writer

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:05.000
Matthew Greenberg was attached to write the
screenplay.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:10.120
Greenberg eventually fell by the wayside and
now only has a story credit on the new film.

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:14.820
Development on the project stalled a bit until
2013, when director Juan Carlos Fresnadillo,

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:18.680
best known for 28 Weeks
Later, was brought on to helm the remake.

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:23.080
Fresnadillo also eventually dropped out, and
development quieted again.

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:27.720
Then, in 2017, directors Kevin Kolsch and
Dennis Widmyer, the team behind the hollywood

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:31.380
horror film Starry Eyes, were hired to tackle
the new adaptation.

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:35.820
Though the new film seems to be very much
in keeping with the spirit of King's original

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:40.110
novel, there's one major change in Pet Sematary
that the filmmakers have already revealed.

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:44.470
In the second trailer for the film, it's revealed
that the Creed child who's killed and resurrected

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:46.840
is the older Ellie, not toddler Gage.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:51.240
The reveal sent a shock through fans expecting
another largely faithful adaptation of the

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:55.570
book, but the filmmakers have some very good
reasons for the change, among them the simple

00:04:55.570 --> 00:04:58.889
fact that an older, and therefore larger child,
is a bigger threat.

00:04:58.889 --> 00:05:03.370
Plus, much of the horror in the 1989 film
revolving around the resurrected Gage was

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:08.110
done with a doll, limiting what the filmmakers
could do if they tried to go that route again.

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:12.720
As producer Lorenzo di Bonaventura put it,
it was an opportunity to show the audience

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:13.720
something new.

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:15.040
He told Entertainment Weekly:

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:35.480
Such a major deviation from Pet Sematary's
original plot is bound to divide fans of both

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:38.020
the source material and the original film.

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:41.950
On one hand, there are purists who would argue
that Gage's death is the natural progression

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:42.990
of the story.

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:46.980
As the most vulnerable member of the Creed
family, his death is the most tragic and his

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:49.360
resurrection is therefore the most horrifying.

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:52.980
On the other hand, there are fans who are
intrigued by the choice, and interested in

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:54.840
seeing how things take shape.

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:58.700
Screenwriter Jeff Buhler admitted that even
he took a little while to come around to the

00:05:58.700 --> 00:05:59.280
idea.

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:22.240
So, what did King think of the big change
to his story?

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:23.920
According to star Jason Clarke:

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:25.700
"Stephen King didn't have an issue with it."

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:30.100
Throughout his career, Stephen King has built
a fictional universe all his own that expands

00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:34.580
to connect, in ways both small and large,
even his most self-contained novels.

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:38.980
This is most overt in his Dark Tower saga,
which deals with the notion of a multiverse,

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:42.110
but even other novels contain these kinds
of lateral references.

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:47.440
The original Pet Sematary novel features references
to Cujo, 'Salem's Lot, and It.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:51.870
With the television series Castle Rock throwing
out King Easter eggs left and right, it makes

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:56.530
sense that the new film would also have its
own list of references for King fans to spot.

00:06:56.530 --> 00:06:58.640
Don't expect them to be too on the nose, though.

00:06:58.640 --> 00:06:59.760
Widmyer told SyFy:

00:07:18.140 --> 00:07:22.760
You can make a very scary, very stylish, and
very violent horror film that still comes

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:23.760
away with a PG-13 rating.

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:28.730
For some filmmakers, that's the way to go,
since it can help the box office if teenagers

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:29.900
are able to see it.

00:07:29.900 --> 00:07:33.630
For other filmmakers, though, there's just
no substitute for pushing the horror all the

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:35.500
way into the rated R territory.

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:38.430
For Pet Sematary, that was always going to
be the right path.

00:07:38.430 --> 00:07:42.350
In some ways, according to di Bonaventura,
it felt like the only path.

00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:43.340
He told SyFy:

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:09.740
Pet Sematary premiered at South by Southwest
this year, making it the second major horror

00:08:09.740 --> 00:08:14.590
release of 2019, along with Jordan Peele's
Us, to claim a key spot at one of America's

00:08:14.590 --> 00:08:16.820
biggest and trendiest film festivals.

00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:21.630
That means that we can get an immediate answer
to the most important question of all: is

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:22.770
it scary?

00:08:22.770 --> 00:08:27.229
Well, according to those who saw it at South
by Southwest, Pet Sematary is most definitely

00:08:27.229 --> 00:08:28.480
a terrifying film.

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:30.040
"It's freaky as hell!"

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:31.980
"Scared the crap outta me!"

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:34.480
Megan Navarro of Bloody Disgusting wrote that:

00:08:55.580 --> 00:08:59.720
You'll get the chance to see how scary the
film is for yourself when it arrives in theaters

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:00.640
on April 5th.

